# Planar Void beside Rest in Peace (rest-in-peace-replaces-the-death-so-the-dies-trigger-never-happens is the same game
# with Rest in Peace): Planar Void's "Whenever another card is put into a graveyard from anywhere, exile that card" is a
# triggered ability (CR 603.2), not a replacement (CR 614.1a), and it has no enters ability. Bot1 Bolts Bot2 on turn 1
# (17), then casts Planar Void on turn 3: the Bolt already in the graveyard stays there. Bot2's Haywire Mite ("When this
# creature dies, you gain 2 life") is Bolted on turn 5: the Mite really dies, so its dies trigger fires (CR 700.4) and
# Bot2 gains 2 (19). While Planar Void's triggers wait on the stack (one for the second Bolt, CR 608.2n, one for the
# Mite), both cards are in their graveyards; then both are exiled.

